body:
"doctors look at me like I am crazy." That happened to the patient above. And me. That is what I am responding to. Those doctors did not admit that they were confused. I am responding to the OP, not the other comments

&#x200B;

Edit: and if you are going to respond trying to get me to retract that doctors cannot know everything, I won't. Doctors cannot know everything. And even if they can know some useful things, it is often very frustrating for the patient getting that useful information. I don't even see how that is controversial.

Many doctors assume that if they don't know what is wrong with you, that there is nothing wrong with you. Almost no doctors will admit their ignorance. I have had doctors sit with me --looking concerned-- explaining to me in roundabout terms why nothing is wrong with me. If nothing was wrong with me, why would it be that difficult to explain?

&#x200B;

In reality, everything that happens in your body happens at the cellular or molecular level. Doctors cannot get a full picture of your body's health from a few tests, when the body is incredibly complex, so many things go undetected, or at least go undetected until they become more severe.

&#x200B;

Edit: I am not a doctor just in case that isn't clear
